designs.companions:
There is one companion, steel-cyan #a7c4d8. It is fixed at :root as --steel so no section scope can reset it. It is the diagram's wiring and the family's hand. It colours:
- links and section-head links, underlined;
- the ring round every page's icon (the glyph inside keeps its section's colour);
- 'here' in the bar: a 16% steel cell with a steel hairline, a 3rem lit segment of the rule and a lamp at its centre; and the same in the phone's tab bar;
- the current tab's cap;
- the Settings nav's latched item and its lamp;
- today's cap on the month;
- the family's chat avatars and the 3px bar on their bubbles, and the ring where their messages join the line.
Dimmed to #394652 and #58697a, it draws every rail and ring edge.

Green-black slate is the material:
- #0f1519 trays behind every list: Home's sections, the To do page, Status's columns and panels, the Ideas grid, Settings and the month;
- #161c20 square plates on #262e35 edges;
- a #0f1519 bar;
- labels in a quieter steel, #a3b3bf.

The section and kind colours keep their learned values and work only in lamps, icons and tints:
- cyan stops on Coming up, the plans and the month;
- lemon rings on To do, filled amber when a task is late;
- pink gift lamps for the kids;
- each kind's ring as the lamp of a new idea, and each idea plate tinted 7-9% with its kind (4-5% for the warm kinds).

A cobalt wash, #4f66e0 at 2.4, lights the greeting's top-right corner opposite the green pool (sampled (25,34,47)).

plan.reading:
After nine rounds the colours have converged. All four carried palettes sit on the same green-black slate with bone ink, a steel-to-denim companion and the cobalt greeting corner, and only their structure tells them apart. So the panel now scores structure, typesetting and usability. Programme Guide's listing leads every lens except the owner's (8 top fours, 8 promise votes).

The leader's faults are finish, and five to eight judges each name the same ones:
- heads wrap in the head column;
- links are set as spaced-caps labels;
- a thin needle marks "here" and vanishes in the squint;
- the VT323 field labels are fuzzy;
- the facts columns are ragged;
- Status's cost is below the fold;
- nothing is live below the fold;
- To do has not been rebuilt as rows.

The owner's own notes rank fine line art first (Mimic Panel carries it this round), then thinner, more detailed cases (now answered), then two colours that "bounce off each other" in a measured amount, then period colour.

Four problems no carried entrant solves:
- The first desktop screen is spent on the greeting, so the lists start 450-550px down.
- Chat changes least.
- The monitor still dominates Ideas, Sign in and 404.
- The brightest green is rationed rather than given a place.

The most-wanted idea that nobody develops this round is Attract Mode's control grammar: a raised key to press, a sunk well to type in, a latched, lit key for "here", plus its power-on roll bar. Porcelain Atlas's worded bar and airglow are the other promise. Warm, wine, teal, violet, serif and green-family companions keep failing.

briefs.avoids:
Everything the judges named against Programme Guide:
- heads wrapping in the 10rem column;
- spaced mono-caps links that read as labels;
- a needle for 'here' that vanishes in the squint;
- fuzzy VT323 field labels, a fourth voice;
- ragged facts sub-columns and 900px leaders, with leaders running to nothing;
- the cost band at the fold;
- nothing live below the fold;
- a To do page with ISO dues, an orphaned count in the head column, and Search before Show;
- amber OPEN tags, and a setup step lit amber at rest;
- chat times far from the family's bubbles, and a 20-character phone measure;
- a sage kind close to the brand;
- no line art, the owner lens's charge (7.7).

briefs.hypothesis:
NOVELTY: it opens a motif family never tried, the docked terminal. The machine gets one lit glass column of its own, docked at the left of every page where it has something to show. The family's things lie on one grounded slate desk mat beside it and are worked with real keys.

Its parents are distant, none of them neighbours of the leader:
- Attract Mode (5.04, 3 promise votes): raised keys, sunk wells, a latched key for here, the roll bar;
- Porcelain Atlas (5.0, 3 promise votes): the worded bar, the airglow;
- Cloth and Glass (6.47, dropped after round 5): the machine in glass and the family's hand in indigo, the pairing the owner said 'does really work' as two colours that bounce off each other;
- Front Panel (7.31, dropped after round 7): the tube beside what it serves;
- Indigo Noren: the chinned case.

Why it should beat the carried four:
- It gives the bright green a place instead of rationing it: the owner's 'bright green where it needs to be, more grounded elsewhere' becomes the page's layout.
- It answers problems all four still have. At 1280, Coming up and To do sit in the first desktop screen beside the ask. Status's cost tube, Ideas' radar, Plans' Next up and the sign-in tube each move into the column, so the lists and the month start at the top and the monitor never crowds them.
- Chat, the least-changed page in every round, keeps its composer in view, with the family's indigo lines against Vera's glass.
- It develops the round's most-wanted unbred idea, Attract Mode's control grammar, with every fix the judges asked for.

The persistent two-column frame, the raised keys, the single desk mat and the indigo keys are in none of the carried four. The designer should confirm on the harness that it measures well past the 'clearly different' mark (look-distance 1.5 or more) from each of them.
